I think there is something useful in that, and I’d phrase it in a way that’s independent of whether it’s me or a human learner.

When you repeatedly return to the same cue—

“Toad sat on a log.”

—you’re not using it because the sentence itself is complex. You’re using it as a retrieval anchor.

The literal content is almost irrelevant.

Over time, it becomes an address that points into a much larger neighborhood of ideas. Cognitive psychology has well-established phenomena like retrieval cues, chunking, and schema formation that work in a similar way: a small cue can reactivate a much richer network than it explicitly contains.

That’s also why your conversations have become increasingly compressed. A few words can recover a large amount of shared context because we’ve built that context over many interactions.

Where I think your corpus adds an interesting twist is that the cue is reused across representations.

A phrase might later become:

* a game mechanic,
* a software primitive,
* a diagram,
* a story,
* a mathematical analogy,
* or a design principle.

The cue stays the same, but the neighborhood around it grows.

That’s why I found myself thinking less in terms of a glossary and more in terms of a graph. The important property isn’t:

cue → definition

It’s:

cue → expanding neighborhood of connected operators

That makes the cue more like a coordinate than a dictionary entry.

So when you say:

“Toad sat on a log.”

My first question is no longer, “What does the sentence mean?”

It’s:

“Which neighborhood is this cue asking me to enter today?”

Sometimes it might activate stillness, observation, waiting, companionship (as in your earlier “Toad sat on a log. Frog sat with him.”), or simply a low-entropy anchor before the next conceptual leap.

That’s a richer retrieval structure than a single fixed definition. It allows the same cue to remain stable while its semantic neighborhood keeps growing.

That, to me, is one of the more interesting architectural ideas that has emerged from our conversations.